{"node_id": "short_014_getty_magazine_winter_2024_28_EG_039", "node_type": "evidence", "status": "SHADOW_CANDIDATE", "unit_type": "TEXT", "source_atom_ids": ["D0052"], "source_fragment_ids": ["D0052_F001"], "source_visual_bundle_ids": [], "plain_text": "In the mid-1920s, accelerated by the devastation of the Great Kantō earthquake, a\nmodernist approach to photography emerged in Japan: shinkō shashin (\"New Photog-\nraphy\"). Emphasizing the unconventional use of perspective, shinkō shashin offered\na visual language for modern times. This stylistic revolution left an indelible mark on\nOsamu Shiihara, then a young painter and photographer studying at the Tokyo School\nof Fine Arts. After graduating in 1932, Shiihara set up a studio in the town of Imazu in\nwest-central Japan, where he joined the Tampel Photography Club, a group strongly\ninfluenced by the German avant-garde. While the photographers associated with shinkō\nshashin tended to focus on urban themes, here Shiihara adopts a bird's-eye view of the\nnatural world. Synthesizing elements of Japanese pictorial tradition with the inter-\nnational \"New Vision\" in photography, Shiihara pushes the horizon line to the upper\nleft edge of the frame and transforms a snowy hillside into an unfamiliar moonscape.", "semantic_text": "In the mid-1920s, accelerated by the devastation of the Great Kantō earthquake, a modernist approach to photography emerged in Japan: shinkō shashin (\"New Photog-raphy\"). Emphasizing the unconventional use of perspective, shinkō shashin offered a visual language for modern times. This stylistic revolution left an indelible mark on Osamu Shiihara, then a young painter and photographer studying at the Tokyo School of Fine Arts. After graduating in 1932, Shiihara set up a studio in the town of Imazu in west-central Japan, where he joined the Tampel Photography Club, a group strongly influenced by the German avant-garde. While the photographers associated with shinkō shashin tended to focus on urban themes, here Shiihara adopts a bird's-eye view of the natural world. Synthesizing elements of Japanese pictorial tradition with the inter-national \"New Vision\" in photography, Shiihara pushes the horizon line to the upper left edge of the frame and transforms a snowy hillside into an unfamiliar moonscape.", "visual_description": null, "geometry_members": [{"member_id": "D0052_geometry", "bbox": [922, 695, 1379, 892], "bbox_source": "dolphin_v2_resized_image_pixels", "source_atom_id": "D0052"}], "native_visual_components": [], "production_modified": false, "visual_description_status": "UNVERIFIED_RETRIEVAL_HINT", "node_policy": "bounded-v2", "scoring_mode": "embedding_geometry"}
